          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>World War I Impact</strong><br>\nThe outbreak of World War I was a turning point for Hesse. Despite his pacifist views, he volunteered for the German army but was declared unfit for service. Instead, he worked with prisoners of war. His anti-war sentiments led to his novels being banned in Germany during World War II, and he faced a backlash from the German public for his views.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Success and Challenges</strong><br>\nThroughout the 1920s and 1930s, Hesse's popularity grew, and he became known for his insightful psychological novels. However, he also faced personal challenges, including several bouts of depression and struggles with marriage and family life. These experiences influenced his writing, which often dealt with the inner turmoil and challenges faced by his characters.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Steppenwolf\" and \"Siddhartha\"</strong><br>\nSteppenwolf and \"Siddhartha,\" published in the 1920s, are considered some of Hesse's most significant contributions to modern literature. \"Steppenwolf\" delves into the duality of human nature, while \"Siddhartha\" portrays the spiritual journey of a young man in ancient India. Both books gained immense popularity, cementing Hesse's status as a leading literary figure.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
