          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1946, Hesse was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature. The prize was given in recognition of his high-quality writing and the lasting impact of his works. The Nobel Committee praised his inspirational representation of the human struggle for understanding and harmony.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hesse's Influence and Legacy</strong><br>\nHesse's influence extends beyond his novels. His writings have inspired countless readers to seek their own path in life and have been a source of comfort to those facing personal challenges. His works continue to be widely read and have been translated into many languages, making him one of the most widely known German-language authors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nHesse spent his later years in Switzerland, where he continued to write until his death on August 9, 1962. His work remained popular throughout his final years and gained a resurgence of interest during the 1960s counterculture movement. Hesse's exploration of individuality and personal growth remains relevant, ensuring his place in the canon of classic literature.</p>\n"
              },
